  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nUpper level ridging moves directly overhead on Tuesday and high \ntemperatures will hold steady or increase by a degree or two. Heat\nheadlines are likely. A few showers or storms are possible again,\nbut overall mostly dry conditions are expected. By Wednesday, the\nridge center will move further east, then flatten, as strong \ntroughing develops over the Upper Midwest. This will force a cold \nfront southward toward the CWA during the day. Ahead of the front,\nstrong heating is likely yet again with highs in the lower 100s \nfor much of the area. Additional heat headlines are likely as heat\nindex values remain in the 105-109 range.\n\nConfidence is increasing that the front will then push into our \narea Wednesday evening into Thursday with increasing rain/thunder \npotential as well as cooling temperatures. Rain chances will \nlinger into Friday before the front lifts back north with \nsoutherly flow returning. High temperatures Thursday through the \nend of the week are now expected to remain below 100 degrees \nareawide...though likely still several degrees above average. For \nsome perspective, the average high in Tulsa this time of year is \n87 and average highs for Fayetteville and Fort Smith are 83 and \n88, respectively. Model solutions vary more significantly this \nweekend into the next, but present potential for one or more \nfronts to impact the area. This could bring additional rainfall \nand even cooler temperatures as we move into mid September. Summer\nhas to end sometime...",
